\section{Choosing a scope}{

\code{scope} decides \strong{what the quintiles are relative to}, which is a
substantive choice, not a technical one.

\describe{
\item{\code{"national"}}{Published national cut-points. Quintile 1 is the
poorest fifth \emph{of the country} at the time of the source survey. In a
targeted or non-representative sample the quintiles will not be evenly
filled -- that is the intended behaviour and usually the finding.}
\item{\code{"urban"}}{Published urban cut-points, for surveys of urban
residents. Position is relative to the national \emph{urban} population.}
\item{\code{"rural"}}{Rural cut-points, where the tool publishes them. Most
tools do not; see the note below.}
\item{\code{"sample"}}{Cut-points computed from your own sample's weighted score
distribution. Quintile 1 is the poorest fifth \emph{of your respondents}, so
the groups are approximately equal in size by construction. Use this when
relative position within the surveyed population is what you want to
report, and say so explicitly -- these are not national quintiles.}
}

Use \code{\link[=et_scopes]{et_scopes()}} to see which published scopes a given tool supports. In the
currently published corpus the 14 "rural + urban" tools provide national and
urban cut-points but no rural ones: the rural score is an intermediate
quantity that is rescaled onto the national scale. For those tools use
\code{scope = "national"} with \code{area}, or \code{scope = "sample"}.
